{"body":"Create a complete 2D side-scrolling horror action game inspired by CARRION, called CARRION: CITY OF CHAOS. The game should feel like a large expansion or DLC for the original CARRION, preserving its distinctive gameplay in which the player controls a terrifying, amorphous alien creature instead of a human protagonist. However, instead of being trapped inside a laboratory, the creature escapes into a massive modern city and begins spreading throughout its streets, buildings, underground tunnels, military installations, shopping centers, hospitals, and industrial districts. The main objective is to allow the player to experience what it would be like to unleash the creature upon an entire city. Preserve the original game's atmospheric pixel art aesthetic, side-scrolling perspective, fluid tentacle movement, organic animations, disturbing sound design, and dark horror atmosphere, while significantly expanding the scale of the environment. Create a large interconnected urban map with multiple districts, including a downtown area filled with skyscrapers, residential neighborhoods, a shopping mall, a subway system, a police station, a military checkpoint, a research facility, a power plant, and an industrial harbor. Every district should have unique architecture, lighting, environmental sounds, enemies, obstacles, and interactive objects. The city should feel alive, with civilians fleeing, emergency vehicles responding, police establishing barricades, soldiers attempting to contain the creature, helicopters searching the streets, and military forces gradually increasing their response as the destruction spreads. The player controls the alien creature using the same general movement style as CARRION, including crawling along walls and ceilings, squeezing through narrow openings, swinging across gaps with tentacles, pulling objects, breaking certain barriers, and navigating through ventilation systems, sewer tunnels, and damaged buildings. The creature must have fluid, organic movement and realistic reactions to the environment. Allow the player to attack human enemies using tentacles, grab them, drag them toward the creature, and consume them to recover biomass and grow stronger. Introduce a dynamic biomass system in which the creature becomes larger and more powerful as it consumes living targets. Different biomass levels should unlock new movement abilities, combat abilities, and destructive attacks. A smaller form should be faster and capable of entering narrow spaces, while larger forms should possess greater strength, longer tentacles, and the ability to smash through reinforced structures. Implement environmental destruction throughout the city. The creature should be able to smash windows, tear down doors, destroy barricades, break certain walls, overturn vehicles, damage streetlights, collapse selected structural elements, and create passages through buildings. Destruction should be visually satisfying, with debris, falling objects, particle effects, screen shake, and environmental reactions. Not every structure needs to be fully destructible, but the environment must react convincingly to the creature's attacks. Allow the player to enter buildings and explore multiple floors connected by staircases, elevators, ventilation shafts, broken walls, and ceilings. Some buildings should contain hidden rooms, secret passages, survivors, security systems, and special biomass upgrades. Add a military response system that evolves according to the creature's activity. Initially, police officers attempt to investigate isolated incidents. As the player causes more destruction, tactical units arrive, roads are blocked, armored vehicles are deployed, and military helicopters begin tracking the creature. Later stages introduce heavily armed containment teams, experimental weapons, automated defense systems, and a specialized military unit designed to capture or eliminate the monster. Enemies should use different tactics depending on their equipment and training. Ordinary police officers should be vulnerable and easily frightened, while trained soldiers should coordinate their attacks, maintain distance, use protective equipment, and attempt to surround the creature. Heavy units should resist basic attacks but move slowly. Helicopters should track the creature from above and attack from a distance, forcing the player to seek cover or use environmental objects strategically. Include boss encounters against a heavily armored containment vehicle, an elite military squad, and an experimental biological weapon created from research on the creature. Each boss should have unique attack patterns, weaknesses, and multiple phases. The creature should be able to use its environment strategically during combat, pulling explosive barrels, throwing vehicles, collapsing platforms, disabling electrical systems, and using ventilation shafts to ambush enemies. Add a citywide panic system that reacts dynamically to the player's actions. Civilians should run away, hide inside buildings, lock doors, or seek shelter in underground stations. Emergency sirens should sound across affected districts, public announcement systems should broadcast evacuation instructions, and news reports should describe the growing disaster. Destroyed areas should remain damaged as the player progresses, creating a persistent visual record of the creature's rampage. Police barricades, abandoned vehicles, broken windows, damaged streets, emergency lights, fires, and debris should gradually transform the city into a devastated environment. Implement a wanted and containment escalation system that tracks the creature's threat level. At low threat levels, only a few officers investigate. At medium threat levels, tactical teams and armored vehicles begin appearing. At high threat levels, the military establishes heavily fortified checkpoints, deploys advanced tracking systems, and attempts to isolate entire districts. At maximum threat level, the city enters emergency lockdown, with military forces attempting a final containment operation. The creature must be able to escape containment through tunnels, rooftops, sewers, damaged buildings, and hidden underground routes. Include a progression system in which the creature unlocks new abilities by consuming special biomass and reaching hidden growth points throughout the city. Possible abilities include extended tentacle reach, reinforced armor, explosive biomass projectiles, temporary camouflage in dark areas, powerful ground slams, rapid movement through narrow passages, and the ability to generate smaller biological tendrils that distract or attack nearby enemies. Balance these abilities so that each has strengths, weaknesses, and appropriate energy or biomass costs. Add a mission system that guides the player through the city without forcing a completely linear experience. Objectives should include escaping the initial containment zone, reaching the subway network, infiltrating a military research center, disabling a citywide containment system, accessing the industrial harbor, and confronting the forces responsible for capturing the creature. Optional objectives should reward exploration and experimentation, including discovering secret laboratories, collecting biological samples, locating hidden passages, and finding abandoned research notes that reveal the creature's origins. The story should begin immediately after the creature escapes from a research facility and reaches the city's outskirts through a drainage tunnel. As the creature explores the city, it discovers that a private military organization has been conducting experiments involving its biological material. The organization intends to capture the creature and use its abilities for military purposes. The player must navigate increasingly dangerous districts, destroy containment infrastructure, and uncover the truth behind the experiments. Offer multiple possible endings depending on the player's choices and discoveries. One ending should involve the creature escaping into the surrounding wilderness. Another should involve the creature taking over the underground infrastructure and establishing a hidden biological nest beneath the city. The most destructive ending should allow the creature to break through the final military blockade and escape into the wider world, leaving the city devastated and its future uncertain. Include several gameplay modes, such as Story Mode, Free Roam, and Survival Mode. Free Roam should allow players to explore unlocked districts and cause destruction without mandatory objectives. Survival Mode should challenge players to survive increasingly intense waves of military containment forces while managing biomass and choosing strategic routes through the environment. Include a detailed sound design with distant sirens, helicopter rotors, gunfire, collapsing structures, frightened voices, emergency broadcasts, electrical failures, creature vocalizations, and disturbing organic sounds. Music should remain atmospheric and adapt dynamically to the threat level, becoming more intense as military forces close in. The user interface should remain minimal and immersive, showing only essential information such as biomass, current abilities, objective markers, and threat level. Make the game playable with keyboard controls and, if supported, touchscreen controls for mobile devices. Prioritize responsive movement, satisfying destruction, intelligent enemy behavior, interconnected exploration, and an atmosphere of escalating urban catastrophe. The finished result must be a genuinely playable game, not merely a visual mockup. Implement functional movement, combat, enemy AI, environmental interactions, destructible objects, progression, missions, and victory and defeat conditions. Begin by creating a polished playable prototype featuring one city district, a controllable creature, destructible objects, human enemies, biomass consumption, and an escalating military response. Once the core gameplay works correctly, expand the city, add new districts, abilities, bosses, missions, and story content. The final experience should capture the unsettling atmosphere and fluid creature control of CARRION while delivering the fantasy of controlling an unstoppable alien organism unleashed upon an entire modern city.The game must feature intense, graphic violence, abundant blood, and disturbing body horror. Do not make the combat look clean, harmless, or cartoonishly sanitized. When the creature attacks humans, include visible blood splatters, blood trails, pools of blood on the ground, and red stains on nearby walls and surfaces. Tentacles should be able to grab, drag, crush, and violently tear apart enemies, with graphic but stylized dismemberment and organic visual effects. When the creature consumes victims, show disturbing animations in which the creature envelops and absorbs their bodies, leaving blood, remains, and biological residue behind. Explosions, powerful impacts, and destructive attacks should produce convincing bloody effects when they hit living targets. The creature's biomass should visibly grow and change as it consumes enemies, reinforcing the unsettling biological horror of the experience. Allow blood and biological residue to persist in the environment, making previously visited areas increasingly disturbing as the creature spreads through the city. Include gruesome death animations, disturbing creature transformations, organic mutations, and unsettling sound effects that emphasize the horror. Maintain a dark, oppressive atmosphere inspired by CARRION, with mature horror presentation and no unnecessary censorship of the game's fictional violence. All gore and blood effects should be implemented as actual gameplay visuals and animations, not merely described in text. The goal is to create a genuinely disturbing, blood-soaked horror experience in which the creature's rampage leaves visible consequences throughout the city.

